KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20 --unwind 4 --no-unwinding-assertions --object-bits 10
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ member-template ctor reconstructing the class pack has its body instantiated: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*dereference failure: pointer NULL in this->invoker.*$
^.*no body for callee.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
KNOWNBUG ([temp.variadic] + [temp.mem]): a member function template of a class
template partial-specialized on a function type R(Args...) whose body uses the
enclosing class's parameter pack Args... to form a template argument (e.g.
reconstructing R(Args...) to instantiate another template) is left with an
uninstantiated (empty) body.  This is std::function's converting constructor
  typedef _Function_handler<_Res(_ArgTypes...), _Functor> _Handler;
  _M_invoker = &_Handler::_M_invoke;
so _M_invoker is never assigned and std::function calls dereference a null
function pointer.  Reclassify CORE once the member template body is
instantiated.

Investigation notes (for the fix):
  * The member ctor body is deferred to typecheck_method_bodies() and elaborated
    by convert_function (cpp_typecheck_method_bodies.cpp ~line 244), wrapped in a
    try/catch that nils the body on failure ("no body for callee").
  * At that point the template_map DOES contain the class pack (pack_args_map has
    Args) and the class/member type params (type_map), so unbound parameters are
    NOT the cause; add_method_body already rebuilds the enclosing class's args
    from ID_C_member_name.
  * handler<R(Args...), F> is instantiated correctly (Args expanded to the
    concrete element types) but then removed as unused -- i.e. the ctor body that
    should reference it is dropped, so the assignment `invoker = &handler<...>`
    never makes it into the converted body (the body comes back empty without a
    hard error in some paths).
  * The member ctor is instantiated more than once and F is deduced as a
    function pointer int(*)(int) (the captureless lambda decays) rather than the
    closure type; the same lambda->function-pointer decay also affects the real
    std::function constructor's _Functor deduction.
  * Net: the gap is in elaborating/keeping the body of a member function
    template (of a class-template partial specialization on a function type) when
    that body reconstructs the class parameter pack into a template argument --
    not in pack binding per se.

